sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-20> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Undecidability has obvious effects even in a very simple system: in a 4-color totalistic cellular automaton (code 1004600) started from simple initial conditions, some patterns die out only after over a thousand steps while others are still growing after ten million steps with no indication of their final outcome.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Undecidability shows up with very simple rules and initial conditions";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Undecidability and Intractability” (from p. 753), p. 755. Scope: 4-color totalistic cellular automaton with code 1004600 and simple programs generally"
}
